title: Converting a tag vocabulary to a hierarchy
tags: [[Hierarchical tags]]
kind: guide
summary: A repository created before October 2026 places its tags with tree_ref: change the schema's constraint, reload it, retype the field — then a tag can take a second parent.

A repository created before October 2026 places its tags with a `tree_ref` `path`,
and its copy of the schema says so (`"type": "tree_ref", "max": 1` on `path`,
[[Schema file]]). It keeps working as it is: `mf tag` writes the type the field has.
To let a tag hang under several parents ([[Hierarchy references]]), convert it:

# In the repository's `.metafolder/config/schema.json`, change the tag group's `path` constraint to `{"field": "path", "type": "hierarchy_ref", "min": 1}` — the new type, and no `max`.
# `mf -n <repo> schema reload`.
# `mf -n <repo> retype path hierarchy_ref` — every tree is a hierarchy, so nothing falls back ([[Changing a field's type]]).

It is one write, undone by `mf log undo` (the schema file is not: put the old
constraint back by hand, or `git checkout` it in `.metafolder/config/` —
[[Repository configuration]]). Then a second parent is an added row:

```
mf -n <repo> metarecord -q 'path = "sport/courir/maraton"' field add path:hierarchy_ref=<évènement's uuid>/maraton
```

or, in the GUI's detail panel on the tag, `metarecord:field add path évènement` — the
new parent alone, typed by its path: the tag keeps its name.

Done in this order: retyped first, the field would break the constraint the schema
still holds. `tests/cli_e2e.rs` (`test_a_tree_vocabulary_converts_to_a_hierarchy`)
runs it.
